Thank you.

515
00:33:31.964 --> 00:33:35.824
We next have Professor Graham Austin, also in person. Welcome.

516
00:33:36.624 --> 00:33:37.003
Thank you.

517
00:33:40.204 --> 00:33:42.584
Graham, welcome. We have five minutes with you this afternoon.

518
00:33:42.644 --> 00:33:43.304
Oh, great.

519
00:33:43.404 --> 00:33:44.804
Thank you for your written submission as well.

520
00:33:45.304 --> 00:33:47.504
Thank you very much to speak with you today.

521
00:33:48.184 --> 00:33:51.764
As you've seen from my written submission, I strongly support

522
00:33:51.774 --> 00:33:55.444
the bill. I think it's the poster child for a

523
00:33:55.484 --> 00:33:59.224
good defense for copyright law. A whole lot of things come together.

524
00:34:00.064 --> 00:34:03.604
It's consistent with the aims of copyright law and that parodies are new works of

525
00:34:03.684 --> 00:34:07.364
authorship, and we want to encourage creativity, and so it

526
00:34:07.404 --> 00:34:11.254
aligns with those basic aims. As other speakers have said, it's a

527
00:34:11.384 --> 00:34:13.504
free speech safety valve,

528
00:34:14.725 --> 00:34:17.995
that these are typically criticism, social commentary, taking the Mickey out of

529
00:34:18.064 --> 00:34:21.364
things. These are good things in a free and democratic

530
00:34:21.665 --> 00:34:22.165
society.

531
00:34:23.245 --> 00:34:27.024
And for a parody to do its job, it has to take quite a lot of the

532
00:34:27.044 --> 00:34:30.214
underlying copyright work. Otherwise, the parody won't work.

533
00:34:30.254 --> 00:34:34.084
And so that's another reason why a defense is needed, and we can't rely on

534
00:34:34.165 --> 00:34:37.784
other parts of the Copyright Act. And as I explained in the written submissions, it

535
00:34:37.824 --> 00:34:41.544
also solves or goes some way to solving a

536
00:34:41.604 --> 00:34:45.354
market failure problem. The Copyright

537
00:34:45.384 --> 00:34:48.924
Act's very clear. It says in Section 14, copyright is a

538
00:34:48.964 --> 00:34:52.944
property right, and in a society like ours, we tend to

539
00:34:53.024 --> 00:34:56.944
say that property owners have the right to decide what happens with their

540
00:34:56.964 --> 00:35:00.944
property, whether they license it to other people, whether they monetize it, and

541
00:35:00.964 --> 00:35:04.784
so on. It's their choice. The problem with parody is that most

542
00:35:04.844 --> 00:35:08.224
people don't like to be lampooned, and so we can't

543
00:35:08.324 --> 00:35:11.944
rely on the copyright owners to enter into a licensing

544
00:35:12.004 --> 00:35:15.844
market for parodies. So this is one of those rare opportunities

545
00:35:16.084 --> 00:35:19.984
or rare instances where it's good for the law to step in and give

546
00:35:20.004 --> 00:35:23.564
people a nudge towards allowing these

547
00:35:23.664 --> 00:35:27.584
parodies. And the final reason is, I think

548
00:35:27.624 --> 00:35:31.574
it brings our laws into line with Australia, Canada, broadly with

549
00:35:31.604 --> 00:35:33.744
the United States, and with the EU.

550
00:35:34.184 --> 00:35:38.084
If you go back to the first reason and say this helps encourage

551
00:35:38.544 --> 00:35:42.394
the creation of new works, if our laws align with other countries, that

552
00:35:42.444 --> 00:35:46.294
takes away some of the transactional friction when people

553
00:35:46.324 --> 00:35:49.624
want to market their parodies into other markets.

554
00:35:51.704 --> 00:35:55.154
We want people to have international distribution deals for their creative work,

555
00:35:55.204 --> 00:35:58.204
and that lowers the friction.

556
00:35:59.804 --> 00:36:03.384
I suspect a number of other submitters have said the bill as currently

557
00:36:03.404 --> 00:36:06.924
drafted is too narrow, and that it needs to

558
00:36:06.944 --> 00:36:10.384
extend to audiovisual works. And I put some

559
00:36:10.424 --> 00:36:12.714
suggestions in the written submissions.

560
00:36:12.744 --> 00:36:16.444
It may be aimed at the officials to help people think through how that might be

561
00:36:16.504 --> 00:36:20.464
achieved in a neat way so that the bill

562
00:36:20.664 --> 00:36:22.804
that comes into select committee is

563
00:36:23.844 --> 00:36:27.344
broadly consistent with the bill that comes out of select committee.

564
00:36:30.004 --> 00:36:33.504
I want to talk about definitions because there has been some discussion about

565
00:36:33.544 --> 00:36:37.484
whether definitions of these concepts are required in

566
00:36:37.544 --> 00:36:41.324
the act. We heard that from Jasmine's submission just a

567
00:36:41.424 --> 00:36:42.084
moment ago.

568
00:36:43.004 --> 00:36:46.920
I don't think that's a good idea. If we are starting A

569
00:36:46.960 --> 00:36:50.780
few years ago, maybe. But in the case law

570
00:36:50.840 --> 00:36:54.750
from other countries, the judges are very good at sorting out what is

571
00:36:54.780 --> 00:36:57.500
a parody, what is a satire.

572
00:36:58.620 --> 00:37:02.330
And our judges and the people who write guidance for creative

573
00:37:02.380 --> 00:37:05.180
workers can draw on that quite easily.

574
00:37:05.210 --> 00:37:08.260
And it might muddy the waters if we try and do this,

575
00:37:08.840 --> 00:37:12.720
and also bring in some inconsistencies in our law, which could

576
00:37:12.800 --> 00:37:16.120
create some of that legal and transactional friction.

577
00:37:16.680 --> 00:37:17.660
So, I think that's

578
00:37:18.680 --> 00:37:22.240
not necessary. And also, we need to remember

579
00:37:22.280 --> 00:37:26.150
that this is fair dealing for the purposes

580
00:37:26.180 --> 00:37:30.040
of parody and satire. And so, that fairness question is

581
00:37:30.100 --> 00:37:33.960
really important. And overseas, judges are really good at

582
00:37:34.040 --> 00:37:37.790
asking what is really the basic question here, which is

583
00:37:38.880 --> 00:37:42.060
should this content be licensed or not?

584
00:37:42.820 --> 00:37:45.260
And that's the basic question here.

585
00:37:45.600 --> 00:37:49.380
It's not so much does it fit into the category of parody or

586
00:37:49.800 --> 00:37:53.240
satire or, in the EU, pastiche, et cetera.

587
00:37:53.680 --> 00:37:57.540
So, I think avoiding definitions, too, is probably

588
00:37:57.550 --> 00:37:58.220
a good thing.

589
00:37:59.520 --> 00:38:03.460
My final point is about moral rights, and there was some discussion

590
00:38:03.520 --> 00:38:06.780
of moral rights. A really good discussion in the first reading of the bill.

591
00:38:08.540 --> 00:38:12.440
In most mature legal systems, moral rights and parody live

592
00:38:12.500 --> 00:38:16.420
together quite happily, and that's also a product of

593
00:38:16.440 --> 00:38:19.980
having parody tightly defined, so that it's very

594
00:38:20.040 --> 00:38:23.230
clear that it's not the original author's work.

595
00:38:23.300 --> 00:38:25.170
It's actually the parodist doing that.

596
00:38:25.180 --> 00:38:29.160
And when that's clear, then it doesn't prejudice the honor and

597
00:38:29.220 --> 00:38:32.120
reputation of the underlying author.

598
00:38:32.480 --> 00:38:36.210
So, I think that the existence of moral rights in our

599
00:38:36.210 --> 00:38:38.680
legislation is not a problem for the successions.

600
00:38:38.820 --> 00:38:42.500
In sum, I'm in favor of it. It's something that's discrete,

601
00:38:43.300 --> 00:38:46.720
and doesn't need to wait for the broader copyright reforms that have been

602
00:38:46.800 --> 00:38:50.760
announced by the government in the wake of the two free trade

603
00:38:50.780 --> 00:38:51.260
agreements.

604
00:38:52.400 --> 00:38:55.219
Might I ask you a really very quick question on that, because we heard last week

605
00:38:55.260 --> 00:38:57.760
from the We Create Alliance, which represents,

606
00:38:59.360 --> 00:39:02.820
I think, 40 of New Zealand's creative industries and organizations representing

607
00:39:02.980 --> 00:39:04.130
30,000 Kiwi creators,

608
00:39:04.960 --> 00:39:05.400
et cetera,

609
00:39:06.220 --> 00:39:10.180
their view was that although change was needed, they thought it

610
00:39:10.200 --> 00:39:12.820
should be through a comprehensive best practice policy development approach.

611
00:39:14.020 --> 00:39:14.460
And a

612
00:39:15.420 --> 00:39:18.770
comprehensive review of the Copyright Act was important,

613
00:39:19.520 --> 00:39:23.150
and their concern of this would be, basically, in

614
00:39:23.180 --> 00:39:26.380
their words, an ad hoc approach to dealing with one discrete issue.

615
00:39:26.780 --> 00:39:26.920
Mm-hmm.

616
00:39:27.040 --> 00:39:27.920
Any comment on that?

617
00:39:29.560 --> 00:39:33.500
Go back to the point, I think that this sits alone and is

618
00:39:33.570 --> 00:39:37.280
needed, and the other kinds of reforms that have been announced,

619
00:39:38.260 --> 00:39:40.340
such as getting rid of the three strikes regime,

620
00:39:41.620 --> 00:39:45.460
the introduction of blocking orders, those kinds of things are not going

621
00:39:45.540 --> 00:39:49.160
to affect the parody and satire

622
00:39:50.880 --> 00:39:53.460
defense that's been proposed in this bill.

623
00:39:53.630 --> 00:39:57.380
So, I think they're separate issues, looking at what

624
00:39:57.580 --> 00:39:58.700
has been announced by the government.

625
00:39:59.660 --> 00:40:00.700
Great. Well, thank you very much for coming-

626
00:40:00.860 --> 00:40:00.990
Thank you

627
00:40:01.000 --> 00:40:03.060
... and making submissions. I have to thank the rest of the commission as well.

628
00:40:03.100 --> 00:40:07.039
Thank you.

629
00:40:07.100 --> 00:40:10.320
Right, that concludes public submissions on this bill this afternoon.

630
00:40:11.140 --> 00:40:12.350
Is there anyone else?
